VALUES
  ('d158','Fluconazole (systemic)','Diflucan, Forcan','Triazole antifungal','Candidiasis (oral/oesophageal/systemic), cryptococcal meningitis, dermatophytoses','Nausea, headache, abdominal pain, hepatotoxicity, QT prolongation','Warfarin, statins, sulphonylureas, phenytoin, QT-prolonging drugs','Concurrent QT-prolonging drugs (e.g., cisapride), severe hepatic impairment, pregnancy (high dose)','Adults: 50-400 mg once daily by indication; cryptococcal meningitis higher doses per protocol. Adjust in renal impairment; monitor liver function on long courses.','D','US-FDA (legacy)',0,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL),
  ('d159','Ketoconazole (topical)','Nizoral','Imidazole antifungal (topical)','Seborrhoeic dermatitis, dandruff, pityriasis versicolor, tinea','Local irritation, dryness; scalp application well tolerated','Minimal topical (oral ketoconazole largely withdrawn due to hepatotoxicity)','Hypersensitivity','Shampoo: apply, leave 3-5 minutes, rinse; twice weekly for 2-4 weeks. Cream: once or twice daily. Topical only — avoid oral ketoconazole.','C','US-FDA (legacy)',1,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL),
  ('d160','Griseofulvin','Grisovin','Antifungal (systemic)','Dermatophyte infections of skin, hair, and nails (tinea capitis)','Headache, nausea, photosensitivity, rash; hepatic effects','Warfarin (reduced effect), oral contraceptives (reduced efficacy), alcohol (flushing)','Severe liver disease, porphyria, pregnancy, systemic lupus erythematosus','Adults: 500 mg-1 g daily with fatty food; tinea capitis in children 10-20 mg/kg/day. Long courses (weeks-months). Use extra contraception.','X','US-FDA (legacy)',0,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL),
  ('d161','Nystatin','Mycostatin, Nystan','Polyene antifungal','Oral and oesophageal candidiasis (thrush), cutaneous candidiasis','Nausea, local irritation; minimal systemic absorption','Minimal','Hypersensitivity','Oral thrush: 1 mL (100,000 units) held in the mouth 4 times daily after food, continue 48 hours after healing. Not absorbed — for local candidiasis only.','A','US-FDA (legacy)',1,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL),
  ('d162','Terbinafine','Lamisil','Allylamine antifungal','Dermatophyte nail infections (onychomycosis), tinea (topical/oral)','Taste disturbance, GI upset, rash, hepatotoxicity (oral)','CYP2D6 substrates (tricyclics, beta-blockers), rifampicin, cimetidine','Chronic/active liver disease (oral); severe renal impairment','Oral: 250 mg once daily for 6 weeks (fingernails) or 12 weeks (toenails). Topical cream once or twice daily. Check liver function for long oral courses.','B','US-FDA (legacy)',1,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL),
  ('d163','Miconazole (oral gel)','Daktarin oral gel','Imidazole antifungal','Oral and oropharyngeal candidiasis, localized intestinal candidiasis','Local irritation, nausea; can potentiate warfarin even topically','Warfarin (enhanced anticoagulation — significant even with oral gel), statins, sulphonylureas','Hypersensitivity; infants under 4 months and those with swallowing difficulty (choking risk)','Apply gel to the affected area 4 times daily after food, retain near lesions before swallowing; continue 7 days after healing. Caution with warfarin.','C','US-FDA (legacy)',1,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL),
  ('d164','Chlorpheniramine','Piriton, Chlorphen','First-generation antihistamine','Allergic reactions, urticaria, allergic rhinitis, adjunct in anaphylaxis','Sedation, dry mouth, blurred vision, urinary retention, paradoxical excitation in children','Alcohol, CNS depressants, other anticholinergics, MAOIs','Narrow-angle glaucoma, prostatic enlargement/urinary retention, neonates','Adults: 4 mg every 4-6 hours, max 24 mg/day; sedating. Caution when driving. Injectable form used as an adjunct in acute allergic reactions.','B','US-FDA (legacy)',1,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL),
  ('d165','Loratadine','Claritin, Lorfast','Second-generation antihistamine','Allergic rhinitis, chronic urticaria, allergic skin conditions','Headache, dry mouth, mild drowsiness (uncommon)','Minimal; CYP3A4 inhibitors may raise levels','Hypersensitivity; reduce dose in severe hepatic impairment','Adults and children over 6: 10 mg once daily. Non-sedating — preferred for daytime allergy relief. Available OTC.','B','US-FDA (legacy)',1,0,'WHO EML 2023; Ghana STG 2017 (AI-drafted — pending pharmacist review)','https://list.essentialmeds.org/',NULL,NULL);
